WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.560
I'm going to give you
just a brief introduction

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.430
to our Google security model.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.763
I'll talk to you a little bit
about some of the security

00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:09.010
products that we've
got in the works.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.062
And I'll also talk
about compliance,

00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:13.520
which will get into a little
bit about what we were talking

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.930
earlier about the MPAA.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.710
So very quickly, when I'm
talking about Google Cloud,

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.300
I'm talking about our compute,
storage, and application

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.615
services.

00:00:22.615 --> 00:00:24.240
And actually, there's
a few more things

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.270
that we've added to this slide
since I created this deck.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.500
But for those of you that
the cloud is a new thing,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.270
we do have our compute
engine and app engine, which

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.800
provide the compute services.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.620
We have a variety of
storage technologies.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.850
Probably Google's cloud storage
and also our persistent disk

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:47.440
capabilities are probably most
interesting to this audience.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.030
And then we have
services like BigQuery,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.210
which you guys can
have a lot of fun

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.680
with if you need to analyze tons
and tons of unstructured data.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.000
Let me talk a little bit
about our security model.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.960
And just so everybody
knows, this slide here--

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:14.970
we did a project about a year
and a half ago with an author

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.510
from "Wired" magazine.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.450
And actually, there's
a really great article

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.360
that "Wired" wrote
about our data centers.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.440
And we had the
pleasure of bringing

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.404
in-- a really great
photographer came in.

00:01:25.404 --> 00:01:27.320
We took him through the
data centers and stuff

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.040
and got a bunch of
beautiful pictures,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.960
so my presentation is sprinkled
with these beautiful pictures.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.370
But they're due to two things.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.949
One is they're really nice,
so it makes me look better.

00:01:39.949 --> 00:01:41.740
But the other thing is
that hopefully it'll

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.615
give you a sense of a
little bit of the scale

00:01:43.615 --> 00:01:46.870
that we're talking about
when we talk about Google.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:51.230
So when you're thinking about--
not even just cloud vendors,

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.270
but when you're
thinking about service

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.230
providers in general--
the types of things

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.920
that a CIO or CSO might
take into consideration

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.030
is what's the technology?

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.250
And what is the technology
that's available to me?

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.500
Is it going to make what
I need to do easier?

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.590
Is it easy to access?

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.050
Is it easy to take advantage of?

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.310
Is it keeping pace, or
is it ahead of the curve?

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.709
And so that's a real big
consideration of why would I

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:19.750
go to a service provider?

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.130
Do they provide me
a better technology?

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.010
And in some instances,
the answer is yes.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.030
In some instances,
the answer is no.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.350
And then quite frankly, the
other reason-- the key reason--

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.280
that anybody would want to take
advantage of a public cloud

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.920
is just the scale of operation.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.510
And can I work
with a vendor that

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:41.130
can provide me the
scale that I need to do

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.240
the jobs that I want to do?

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.120
And can they provide me
that scale at something

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.400
that's competitive
with doing it myself?

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.550
And then also, how
is that vendor?

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.260
Are they agile?

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.468
Can they keep up with things?

00:02:55.468 --> 00:02:57.100
I mean, from a
security point of view,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.680
are they keeping up with
the threat environment?

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.180
Are they doing the
right types of things?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.500
And are they doing
this in a manner

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.080
that I don't have
to worry about it?

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.750
And can they do it
faster than my staff?

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.740
And so these are all
kinds of considerations

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.820
that we've encountered
as we've talked

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.900
to customers across a wide
variety of industries.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.965
But these seem to be the things
that come to front of mind

00:03:14.965 --> 00:03:16.840
as people are thinking
about taking advantage

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.620
of Google's Cloud Platform.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.090
So the good news is-- to
answer those questions-- is

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.460
that the environment--
the Cloud Platform

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.900
that we're making
available to our customers

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.360
is the platform that
we use ourselves.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.730
And we use this platform
to run our global search

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.230
and advertising business.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.410
Our Google Apps
business runs on this.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.850
Our YouTube business
runs on this.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.000
This is the
infrastructure that we

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.640
are servicing our
hundreds of millions

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.300
of users across the globe.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.680
And so this is the real deal.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.690
It's not something
special we've built just

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.125
to build a Cloud Platform.

00:03:56.125 --> 00:03:58.250
And there's some really
interesting characteristics

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.531
about it.

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:03.670
For us, security is
a basic principle

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.160
of what we think about, because
we do use this environment

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.280
to run our own business.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.970
We want to protect
that business.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.659
The search and
advertising business

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:15.200
is a pretty good
business for Google.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.410
And so we have security as a
foundational thought in terms

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.980
of building our
infrastructure, and services

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.490
on top of that infrastructure.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.940
And so some of the
things that are

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:28.369
kind of interesting
about it is that we

00:04:28.369 --> 00:04:30.660
build our own servers and
our own networking equipment.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.570
And in fact, we're one
of the top five server

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.280
manufacturers on the globe.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.290
But those servers that we build
are exclusively for own use.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.980
And the interesting
thing about it

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.660
is that we've got largely
a homogeneous fleet.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.842
And you might think, oh,
well why is that important?

00:04:46.842 --> 00:04:48.550
Well, the thing that's
really interesting

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.270
there is that that enables
us to maintain the fleet

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.460
in a couple of dimensions.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.520
One is it's very clear to us
what the hardware and software

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.530
profile looks like on
that fleet, because it's

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.460
the same across the whole fleet.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.780
And the other thing is
that as a result of that,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.910
we're able to react to
things like patch management

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:08.660
and dealing with vulnerabilities
much, much quicker than we

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.685
would if our tens of
thousands of servers

00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:12.310
were all different
versions of software

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.690
and different hardware
profiles and things like that.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:19.870
So it's a simplicity idea
which enables us to do things

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.392
at very large scale.

00:05:21.392 --> 00:05:23.850
We have dozens of data centers
spread around the globe that

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.599
enable us to deliver
uninterrupted services to all

00:05:26.599 --> 00:05:27.307
of our customers.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.570
We protect our data at rest.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:32.790
We protect it in transit.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:37.090
We use encryption technologies
to help us take care of that.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.750
One of the other things that's
very interesting about Google

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:44.110
as a service provider, or
as just a commercial entity,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.614
is that Google is very
interesting in that we

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:50.780
are disciplined in our
software development process.

00:05:53.326 --> 00:05:55.490
And through that,
we're able to do--

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.820
we do some things
as best practices

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.690
which help us deliver
more secure services.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.520
And things that we do there
is, for example, all services

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.630
that we build get security
and privacy design reviews

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.730
ahead of any code being written.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.600
All code that's written
by a Google engineer

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.560
is at least reviewed
by a peer before it's

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.930
checked into our
source code repository.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:20.450
And we also have a separation
between our development

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:24.260
operation and our actual
operations of the services.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.490
And in fact, another
kind of fun fact

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:34.910
is that software services that
we deliver into production come

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:37.240
through a certified
build system,

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.150
and we actually validate that.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:42.010
And so you can't actually go
into our production service

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.890
and patch anything unless
it goes through our

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.090
build service and is certified.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.750
So there's no way for a
development engineer sitting

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.134
somewhere in Google to
actually insert code

00:06:51.134 --> 00:06:52.550
into our production
systems that's

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.660
not supposed to be there.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.035
We also take advantage of
external security verifications

00:06:57.035 --> 00:06:58.940
and validations.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.250
We'll run the whole
gamut of we use

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.400
commercial scanners to
scan our services looking

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.540
for vulnerabilities.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.850
And we also will
take a step back

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.970
and we will bring in
independent security verifiers

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:13.080
to actually go through
source code reviews with us

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.010
and help us look at things that
we really want to truly harden

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:20.540
and we want to take
an unbiased look at.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.650
As Dave was saying
earlier, we have

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:27.200
over 500 personnel
that are specifically

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.370
involved in security
and privacy at Google.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.790
And many of these
people are actually

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.430
experts in their field.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:40.570
And they continue to do
security research and publish.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.890
They continue to
blog and comment

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.330
on the security industry.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.865
And they do tons
of presentations

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:49.350
at a variety of
conferences, everything

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:55.060
from Black Hat to RSA and
everything in between.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.214
We also have one thing that
we're really proud of--

00:07:59.214 --> 00:08:01.630
and this is something that we
started in the industry-- is

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.710
we have our vulnerability
rewards program,

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.610
where we actually pay bounties
to security researchers

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.280
to find problems
in our software.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.500
And for example, this
particular program

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:16.390
found over 700 vulnerabilities
or bugs in the Chrome browser

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:18.850
over the lifecycle
of its development.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:21.400
And the program has
paid out over $2 million

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.600
to date to security researchers.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.530
And oftentimes, the bounties
are $10,000, $20,000 a pop.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:32.309
So you can imagine some grad
student working on his PhD

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:37.850
is able to live for a year
off finding a bug at Google.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.850
I fortunately don't eat
that much ramen anymore,

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:41.640
so I'm a little
bit more expensive.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.250
But some of them
really enjoy it.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.730
And the other thing we do is
we give them credit for it.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.760
And so for those researchers
that find those things,

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.310
we make sure that the world
knows that they did us

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.830
a favor, which helps them out
as they're building up their CVs

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.080
and looking to move
beyond academia.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.650
We have a global network
to deliver our services.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.520
And our customers of
the Cloud Platform

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.750
can also take advantage of this.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.320
One of the other things that
we're particularly proud of

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:14.650
is that we believe that we've
provided a lot of leadership

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:17.620
in security innovation
across the industry.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.630
And doing things like ensuring
that all the email that

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.590
comes out of Gmail
and comes into Gmail

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.320
is protected with
Transport Layer Security.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.652
We've gone to HTTPS on
all of our services.

00:09:32.396 --> 00:09:34.410
We were one of the
first providers

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.910
to get the ISO 27001
certification on our Google

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.000
Apps services, our SaaS offering
for collaboration applications.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:49.260
And we have some other things
that we continue to do.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.080
We recently put out--
or a couple years ago--

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:56.900
and we've done TLS on Gmail.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:00.820
And we've recently
published a safe email site

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:04.180
which has identified the
service providers who are not

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.720
taking advantage of TLS.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.390
And the interesting
side effect of that

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:11.890
is that some service
providers, who will go unnamed,

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.930
quickly upgraded their
services once we shined

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.657
a little sunlight on that.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.450
So let me shift gears
here just a moment.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.595
If you're thinking
about IT security

00:10:25.595 --> 00:10:27.220
or you're thinking
about your services,

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.640
you're thinking about using a
vendor, what kinds of things

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:31.970
are you worried about?

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.000
So I'm going to take a couple
moments here and just give you

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.060
guys a taste of the threat
model or the environment

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.210
that we're thinking about.

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.335
And also what we've learned
from a lot of customers

00:10:41.335 --> 00:10:42.829
that we've talked about.

00:10:42.829 --> 00:10:44.620
And just in case you're
curious about this,

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.820
this is the crew at our
data center in Finland.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:48.670
They're getting out.

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.034
Getting a little sunshine here.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.650
So who are you going
to worry about?

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:59.490
Here's the spectrum of
folks that we worry about.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.980
Hackers.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.760
We worry about-- if
we're a customer,

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.850
we're worried about
the service provider.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:07.360
We worry about
our own personnel,

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.410
and in the shoes
of our customers,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.130
they can worry about
themselves as well.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.629
And we also worry
about governments.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.200
Let's dig in a little bit here.

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:23.480
So hackers.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:28.520
So we have a lot of
mechanisms we've put in place.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:31.670
As you can well imagine,
Google's a very large target.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:37.280
And we have persistent
attacks going on.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:38.810
It's been going on for years.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:43.870
And the hacking community has
had very, very little success

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.865
in being successful
against Google.

00:11:46.865 --> 00:11:48.490
But the interesting
thing is because we

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.520
do have such a wide footprint,
and we do see a lot of traffic

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.330
across the globe, it enables
us to get smarter and smarter

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:55.310
our countermeasures.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:57.582
And we build those
things into our front end

00:11:57.582 --> 00:11:59.540
services and the things
that face the internet.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.080
And we learn about
a lot of threats.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.621
And our customers get to take
advantage of those protections.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:07.490
Well, what about us?

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:09.780
What about Google as
the service provider?

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:13.830
I already described our software
development process to you.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.810
We also employ a least privilege
model in terms of access

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.890
to customer data,
which there are

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.070
VPs in the company that are
responsible for granting

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:22.680
that access.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.070
And we also perform background
checks on our employees.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:32.140
And we have a security and
privacy focused culture.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.310
We have public information
on our investor site

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:38.000
that explains the
employee code of conduct,

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.650
which employees agree to when
they start employment with us.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:48.350
And we refresh the privacy and
security information training

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:50.471
to our employees
on an annual basis.

00:12:50.471 --> 00:12:52.970
And we do this top to bottom,
every employee in the company.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.125
The other things that are
hallmarks of-- contrary

00:13:00.125 --> 00:13:02.290
to what you might read
in the press and stuff--

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.050
is that as a customer
of Google, it's

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.140
very clear that
you own your data

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:08.490
and you are the
controller of that data.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.880
Google operates in a
role of a data processor

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.630
and follows your instructions.

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.970
We're not sharing this
information with anybody else.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:19.400
We're not using it for
our other businesses.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:23.560
We use your data to provide the
service to you in the manner

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:25.680
that you want it.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.060
As I said, we have
strict internal policies

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:33.820
that follow up on the privacy
and security training.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:35.317
You know, I'll be really honest.

00:13:35.317 --> 00:13:36.900
We've fired people
for violating that.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.540
We've shown them the
door very quickly.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.080
We take it very seriously.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.000
And we also verify what we say.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.250
We verify our policies.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.291
We track and audit our
employees' activities

00:13:46.291 --> 00:13:48.290
to make sure that we're
doing what we say we do.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.530
As a customer, we provide you
control over your own data.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:14:02.400
And so this is an example of
one of our user interfaces.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.810
It's actually a
little bit dated.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.351
But what I'm trying
to demonstrate to

00:14:05.351 --> 00:14:07.784
you here is that
this is how we give

00:14:07.784 --> 00:14:08.950
the control to the customer.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:10.010
They control the access.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.160
They control who uses it.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.340
They control which
services are being used.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.010
And so all of that
control is placed

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:16.261
in the hands of customers.

00:14:16.261 --> 00:14:18.010
We do have a customer
support organization

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.500
that'll help you out if you
run into trouble with that.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.447
But you are using
these resources

00:14:22.447 --> 00:14:23.780
and controlling them yourselves.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.210
Let's talk about governments.

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:35.090
And this becomes very
interesting in that-- I don't

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.650
know if it really applies
as much to the media

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.400
and entertainment
industry as it does some

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.070
of the other industries,
but we always

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.320
have customers
that are concerned

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:46.120
about governments
getting access to data

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.030
that's stored in our cloud.

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:50.850
And so a couple
things about that.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:52.740
One is you may or
may not be aware,

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:54.550
but Google was the
first to publish

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.910
what we call a government
transparency report.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.510
And so we are
publishing information

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.430
about all the
information requests

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.866
we get from the US
federal government

00:15:03.866 --> 00:15:05.490
and other governments
around the world.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.220
In our terms of service--
or in our agreements

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.630
with the customers--
our customers

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:17.682
are responsible for dealing
with government requests.

00:15:17.682 --> 00:15:19.640
And it's our policy to
make sure that whoever's

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:21.931
making that request really
understands that, and really

00:15:21.931 --> 00:15:23.810
interfaces with the customer.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.270
And in the situations where
we were that doesn't happen

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:29.820
and we can't do that, unless
we're compelled by law,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.600
it's also our policy to make
sure that the customer is aware

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.410
that that activity's going on.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:40.120
And we push back on
that to make sure

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.400
that it's very
appropriately legal when

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.490
those types of things do happen.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.230
We're also Safe
Harbor certified.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:52.700
And what that means is
that enables us to comply

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:55.090
with European privacy law.

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:58.650
And we can present that
to our European customers

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:04.500
so that they understand that
and gives us legal standing.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.310
And you don't have to
just take our word for it.

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:10.560
This is last year's scorecard,
presented by the Electronic

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:11.910
Frontier Foundation.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:13.590
They do an annual
survey that they

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:15.270
call "Who's Got
Your Back?" where

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:19.140
they rate the various vendors
in terms of how they're

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:21.700
dealing with handling the
privacy of their customers'

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:22.770
information.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:25.710
And as you can see in the
middle of the slide there,

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.344
we scored very well with them.

00:16:27.344 --> 00:16:29.510
The other thing that's
really interesting about this

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:32.839
is that we've
improved over time.

00:16:32.839 --> 00:16:34.630
We haven't always gotten
the highest marks.

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.210
But the other thing that's
really nice about this

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.120
is that the Electronic
Frontier Foundation does

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:39.750
this on an annual
basis, but they also

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.770
adapted to the
changing environment.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:44.340
And so they're
constantly pushing

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.750
on what they're
measuring everybody on,

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:48.820
so it's not just a static thing.

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:52.110
You have to pay attention to
what they're paying attention

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:53.652
to, and we try to closely.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:02.380
OK, let's talk about compliance.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:06.079
So not specific to
media and entertainment,

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:11.190
we do run a formal compliance
program for Cloud Platform.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:13.839
And what we're trying
to do there is we're

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:19.180
trying to, in industry specific
terms, convey to our customers

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:21.480
across the broad
spectrum of industries

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.640
that were complying with
security and privacy standards

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.280
in those different industries.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.620
And to that effect, we actually
use third party auditors

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:34.560
to attest to most of these
things where it's appropriate.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:38.860
And so just from an
IT service provider

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:44.080
point of view, the SSAE
16 and the ISO 27001

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:48.252
are two very important standards
there, which we bring in.

00:17:48.252 --> 00:17:49.960
We have our third
party auditor, which is

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:51.520
Ernst &amp; Young, certified point.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:54.350
They come in and do these audits
for us on an annual basis.

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:58.267
And we can provide
materials that back that up

00:17:58.267 --> 00:17:59.850
and show the evidence
to our customers

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:01.170
that we're doing that.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:05.490
And so this has served us very
well in some other industry

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.100
verticals.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:10.930
ISO 27001 is a pretty rigorous
security and privacy standard,

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:12.520
focused mostly on IT security.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.290
And there's 12
control areas there,

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:17.630
and we meet all of
those control areas

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:21.030
and can make the certificate
available to customers.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.930
Based upon that,
we built upon that

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:27.000
and we're able to service
the health care industry

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.670
by complying with HIPAA.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.200
And we comply with HIPAA.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.690
And we'll meet the
contractual requirements

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:36.570
by providing a business
associate's agreement

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:39.544
to covered entities in
the health care space.

00:18:39.544 --> 00:18:41.460
I know that sounds like
a lot of gobbledegook.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:46.080
But what that means is that if
you are building a health care

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:50.450
application which has
patient data in it,

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.600
that data is covered
by the HIPAA laws.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:55.040
And we can provide
an environment

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:57.640
that enables
customers to deliver

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:00.100
those type of applications
but be sure that the service

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.500
provider, us, is making
sure that that data stays

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:04.721
safe and private.

00:19:04.721 --> 00:19:06.470
And we're backing that
up in the contract.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:15.600
And in a very similar
vein, we support PCI DSS,

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.710
which is the payment card
industry's data security

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:19.050
standard.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:22.620
We've certified the whole
platform against version 2.

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.720
And the reason it's
version 2 is at the time

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:27.840
we started the audit
on that, version 3

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.280
hadn't been ratified by the
payment card industry yet.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.050
They did that work,
and we actually

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:36.410
have just completed
our 3.0 certification

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.606
and we're signing
certificates for right now.

00:19:39.606 --> 00:19:41.230
So we're very shortly--
in fact, you're

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.540
the first people that are
hearing this right now,

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.600
because I got the
email this morning.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:48.340
But we'll be making
some noise about that.

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:51.197
So for those folks in
the retail industry

00:19:51.197 --> 00:19:52.780
and the people in
the banking industry

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.740
that are dealing with
payment card data,

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.570
we provide this assurance
that we'll safeguard

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:58.980
that information as well.

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:03.060
And they can use that to build
up their compliance story

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.740
with their auditors and
with their customers

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.220
that they need to do that with.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:10.420
Very quickly, FISMA and FedRAMP.

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:14.350
These are US federal government
standards for government

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:18.070
to be able to take advantage
of information services.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.910
We've done FISMA
where appropriate.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:24.824
FedRAMP has replaced that,
and it's currently in process.

00:20:24.824 --> 00:20:27.240
We have a goal of getting that
done by the end of the year

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.610
so we can tap into those
US federal government

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.480
customers that require that, and
the vendors that support them

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:33.610
as well.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.280
And then I have
this dream out here

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:40.190
that I call the MPAA, which is
really relevant to this crowd.

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:44.220
And so I'll just give
you-- the story on this

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.710
is that we were paying
attention to it.

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.870
We looked at the first
version of the standard.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.610
We were like, OK, this
isn't a really-- this

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:53.230
is pretty straightforward.

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:56.330
We think we can meet or exceed
the requirements in here.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:59.010
But as we started to get to
know people in the industry

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.730
and stuff, and we learned
a little bit more,

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:03.580
everybody was kind
of like, well, OK,

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:07.910
that's not really-- that
doesn't do anything for us.

00:21:07.910 --> 00:21:10.200
And not only did we
hear that, but I think

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:11.910
the MPAA heard that as well.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:12.920
And so they've gone off.

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:14.820
And I think there's several
people in this room that

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:15.996
have been involved in that.

00:21:15.996 --> 00:21:17.370
And we've talked
with some people

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:18.950
at the major studios
that have been

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:20.450
involved with that as well.

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:22.430
And they're retooling that.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:25.810
And they're retooling that to
think more about cloud services

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:29.780
and some more complex
things that could be done.

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:33.100
And so we're anxiously awaiting
them to publish that standard,

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:37.440
so that we can communicate
an assessment against that

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:40.170
to everybody.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:43.160
So that's an activity
that we have going on.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:47.157
And hopefully that'll
be something--

00:21:47.157 --> 00:21:48.740
it's probably not
the be all end all--

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.290
but hopefully
that'll be something

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:53.940
that helps you guys to
be able to take advantage

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:54.920
of our platform.

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:57.150
And our competitors'
platforms, to be quite frank.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.800
Hopefully that will
give an inch away.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:04.420
Aside from that, we are
also trying to figure out

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:07.060
and trying to navigate
the process of working

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.170
with the studios, and really
trying to listen and understand

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.020
what the studios
want to understand.

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:14.450
What we can do to demonstrate
to them that we're

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:17.180
meeting or exceeding their
expectations in terms

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:20.140
of protecting their data, and
enabling their vendors to take

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:23.070
advantage of our services and
protect their data as well.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:28.000
And so we're hoping in
the not too distant future

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:32.050
that we'll be able to
tell a richer story there,

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:34.940
so that there can be
broader use of our platform

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:35.850
in your industry.

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:44.270
Very quickly, one of the
other things that we're doing.

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:46.920
We talked about having
these over 500 folks that

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.510
work on security at Google.

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:52.120
We've built up a
pretty rich tool set

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.650
inside of our
security organization

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:56.330
that we use on our own products.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.270
And one of the things that
I'm in the process of doing

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.550
is I'm in the process
of commercializing

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:02.820
some of these products.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:06.600
And this is an example of the
first one that is coming out.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.460
This is called Cloud
Security Scanner.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:10.410
What this does is
this is a tool that

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:14.370
works in conjunction with
applications that are developed

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:15.920
on Google App Engine.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:19.520
And it basically will scan
that for common web development

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:22.420
vulnerabilities cross-site
scripting errors, DOM problems.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.840
And this is a tool
we use on our own.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.260
And all products that
we launch to the web

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.892
have to pass through this tool
or they can't get launched.

00:23:29.892 --> 00:23:31.600
And so what we've done
is we've made this

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:32.500
available to our customers.

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:33.458
It's currently in beta.

00:23:33.458 --> 00:23:35.410
It's available in the
developer's console.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:38.216
And you can run it against
your own applications,

00:23:38.216 --> 00:23:39.340
and you get feedback on it.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.200
And you can fix the bugs.

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:43.680
And part of what we're
trying to do here

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.820
is make our platform
a really logical place

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:49.140
to deliver safe and
secure applications,

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:51.910
and let you guys take
advantage of years of expertise

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:54.170
we have in dealing with that.

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:57.860
So lastly, what I
want to convey here

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.560
is let us worry
about the security,

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.980
so that you guys can innovate
and take advantage of that,

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:07.570
and take off and really
focus on what you want to do.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:08.780
Focus on creative things.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:12.120
Focus on innovative software,
innovative applications.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:16.160
And let us help you with
the security side of things.

